    <h1>1. Measuring sets</h1>
    <p>A macroeconomic model says things like “6 percent of households are at their borrowing limit,” “aggregate capital is the sum of everyone's savings,” and “the household forms expectations given what it knows today.” With ten households and a coin toss, each of these is arithmetic. With a continuum of households, a shock that can take any real value, and a future that runs forever, each one needs a definition. Measure theory supplies those definitions. This chapter builds the first two pieces: a way to assign a size to a set, and a description of which sets are allowed to have a size. The second piece turns out to be the mathematical form of “information,” which is the reason economists need it at all.</p>
    <p><strong>What is assumed.</strong> Calculus of one and several variables, including the Riemann integral $\int_a^b f(x)\,dx$ as area under a curve. Probability at the level of a first course: discrete distributions with probabilities $p_k$, continuous ones with a density, expectation, independence. Sequences and limits: $x_n \to x$ means $x_n$ gets and stays arbitrarily close to $x$. Nothing from a real analysis course is assumed, and every symbol beyond these is defined before it is used.</p>

    <h2 id="questions">1.1 Three questions a macro model asks</h2>
    <p><strong>How many households?</strong> Heterogeneous-agent models (Huggett, Aiyagari, Krusell–Smith, HANK) put a continuum of households on the interval $[0, 1]$, one household for each number $i$ in it. Household $i$ holds assets $a(i)$. The model then speaks of “the fraction of households with assets below $1$,” which must mean the total length of the set of $i$ with $a(i) \lt 1$. If $a$ is a nice function, that set is a union of a few intervals and its length is obvious. But $a$ is the output of an optimisation problem, and nothing guarantees the set is a union of intervals. We need a notion of length that applies to more complicated sets, and we need to know which sets can be given a length.</p>
    <p><strong>How likely is a history?</strong> Productivity follows $z_{t+1} = \rho z_t + \varepsilon_{t+1}$, with a fresh normally distributed shock $\varepsilon_{t+1}$ every period, forever. A history is an infinite sequence $(\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2, \varepsilon_3, \ldots)$. “Productivity eventually exceeds $3$” is a statement about a whole history. Any single history has probability zero, so these probabilities cannot be found by adding up probabilities of individual outcomes as in a discrete course. We need probabilities for sets of histories directly.</p>
    <p><strong>What does the household know?</strong> The Euler equation $u'(c_t) = \beta\, \mathbb{E}_t\bigl[(1 + r_{t+1})\, u'(c_{t+1})\bigr]$ uses $\mathbb{E}_t$, the expectation “given information at date $t$.” Information has to be a mathematical object for this to mean anything.</p>
    <p>The answers, in order: a <em>measure</em> gives sets a size (this chapter); a <em>σ-algebra</em> says which sets can be sized, and doubles as a description of information (this chapter); the <em>integral</em> turns sizes into averages and aggregates (Chapter 3); <em>conditional expectation</em> is averaging given information (Chapter 5).</p>

    <h2 id="notation">1.2 Notation for sets</h2>
    <p>Everything in these notes happens inside one fixed set, written $\Omega$ (capital omega). In probability, $\Omega$ is the set of all possible states of the world or histories, and an element $\omega \in \Omega$ is one particular history. In a model with a continuum of agents, $\Omega$ might instead be $[0, 1]$, the set of agents. The symbols:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>$\omega \in A$: the element $\omega$ belongs to the set $A$. $A \subseteq B$: every element of $A$ is in $B$. $\varnothing$ is the empty set.</li>
      <li>$A \cup B$ (union): elements in $A$ or $B$ or both. $A \cap B$ (intersection): elements in both. $A \setminus B$: elements of $A$ not in $B$.</li>
      <li>$A^c = \Omega \setminus A$, the complement: everything in $\Omega$ not in $A$. If $A$ is the event “a recession occurs,” $A^c$ is “no recession occurs.”</li>
      <li>For a sequence of sets $A_1, A_2, \ldots$, the union $\bigcup_{n=1}^\infty A_n$ is the set of elements in <em>at least one</em> $A_n$, and $\bigcap_{n=1}^\infty A_n$ the set of elements in <em>every</em> $A_n$. Sets are <strong>disjoint</strong> if no two of them share an element.</li>
      <li>De Morgan's laws: $\bigl(\bigcup_n A_n\bigr)^c = \bigcap_n A_n^c$ and $\bigl(\bigcap_n A_n\bigr)^c = \bigcup_n A_n^c$. “Not (at least one happens)” is “every one fails.”</li>
      <li>$\{x : P(x)\}$ is the set of all $x$ with property $P$. For instance $\{i \in [0,1] : a(i) \lt 1\}$.</li>
      <li>$\mathbb{N} = \{1, 2, 3, \ldots\}$, $\mathbb{Q}$ the rationals (fractions $p/q$), $\mathbb{R}$ the real line. $[a, b]$ includes its endpoints, $(a, b)$ excludes them, and $(a, b]$ includes only $b$.</li>
      <li>The <strong>indicator</strong> of a set $A$ is the function $\mathbf{1}_A(\omega) = 1$ if $\omega \in A$ and $0$ otherwise. It converts sets into functions, and it will be the bridge from measuring sets to integrating functions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A set is <strong>countable</strong> if its elements can be listed as a sequence $x_1, x_2, x_3, \ldots$ (finite sets count as countable). $\mathbb{N}$ is countable by definition. The integers are countable: $0, 1, -1, 2, -2, \ldots$. The rationals are countable too, though this is less obvious: list fractions by the size of numerator plus denominator, skipping repeats. The interval $[0, 1]$ is <strong>uncountable</strong>: Cantor proved that every list of real numbers misses some real number. The line between countable and uncountable is where measure theory draws its rules.</p>
    <p>Finally, for a set $S$ of real numbers, $\sup S$ (the supremum) is its least upper bound: the smallest number that is $\geq$ everything in $S$. It need not belong to $S$: $\sup\,(0, 1) = 1$. Similarly $\inf S$, the infimum, is the greatest lower bound. When $S$ is unbounded above, $\sup S = \infty$. Sizes in these notes take values in $[0, \infty]$, the nonnegative reals together with $\infty$, with the conventions $x + \infty = \infty$ and, later, $0 \cdot \infty = 0$.</p>

    <h2 id="size">1.3 What a notion of size should do</h2>
    <p>Start with length on the real line. An interval $(a, b]$ has length $b - a$. Length of other sets should follow some rules. Writing $\mu(A)$ for the size of $A$ (the Greek letter mu, for measure):</p>
    <ol>
      <li>$\mu(A) \geq 0$, and $\mu(\varnothing) = 0$.</li>
      <li>If $A$ and $B$ are disjoint, $\mu(A \cup B) = \mu(A) + \mu(B)$.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Rule 2 extends to any finite number of disjoint sets. The decisive question is whether it should extend to infinitely many. Here is why it must, at least for a sequence. Toss a fair coin repeatedly. Let $A_n$ be the event “the first head occurs on toss $n$,” so $\mu(A_n) = 2^{-n}$. The events are disjoint, and their union is “a head eventually occurs.” Finite additivity can only say that the union has probability at least $\frac12 + \frac14 + \cdots + 2^{-N}$ for every $N$. It never pins the answer down. Every economist would say the answer is $1$, which is the sum of the whole series. That conclusion is the rule</p>
    $$
    \mu\Bigl(\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n\Bigr) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(A_n) \qquad \text{for disjoint } A_1, A_2, \ldots
    $$
    <p>called <strong>countable additivity</strong>. It is what makes “eventually,” “infinitely often,” and “in the limit” computable, and every limit argument in these notes leans on it. The same reasoning gives the probability that an unemployed worker eventually finds a job, or that a borrowing constraint is ever hit.</p>
    <p>Additivity cannot be pushed to <em>uncountable</em> unions. The interval $[0, 1]$ is the union of its points, each of length $0$. If sizes added over uncountably many disjoint pieces, the length of $[0,1]$ would be a sum of zeros, which is $0$ rather than $1$. So countable additivity is not an arbitrary choice: it is the most additivity that is consistent with length.</p>

    <h2 id="sigma">1.4 Which sets get a size: σ-algebras</h2>
    <p>It would be convenient to give every subset of $[0,1]$ a length. That turns out to be impossible.</p>
    <p><strong>Theorem</strong> (Vitali, 1905). There is no function $\mu$ defined on <em>all</em> subsets of $[0, 1)$ that is countably additive, gives every interval its length, and is unchanged by shifting a set along the circle (sliding it right and wrapping around at $1$).</p>
    <p>The proof constructs a very strange set using the axiom of choice: a set that picks exactly one point from each class of numbers differing by a rational. Countably many shifted copies of it tile $[0,1)$ exactly, so its length would have to be a number $x$ with $x + x + x + \cdots = 1$, and no such number exists. Nobody can write such a set down explicitly, and no economic model ever produces one. But the theorem means we must decide in advance which sets will be measured. We want a collection that is closed under everything we will do to sets: complements, and countable unions and intersections.</p>
    <p><strong>Definition.</strong> A <strong>σ-algebra</strong> on $\Omega$ is a collection $\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $\Omega$ such that</p>
    <ol>
      <li>$\Omega \in \mathcal{F}$;</li>
      <li>if $A \in \mathcal{F}$, then $A^c \in \mathcal{F}$;</li>
      <li>if $A_1, A_2, \ldots \in \mathcal{F}$, then $\bigcup_{n=1}^\infty A_n \in \mathcal{F}$.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The “σ” (sigma) signals the word countable, as in a countable sum $\Sigma$. The pair $(\Omega, \mathcal{F})$ is a <strong>measurable space</strong>, and the sets in $\mathcal{F}$ are its <strong>measurable sets</strong>, called <strong>events</strong> in probability. Other closure properties follow. $\varnothing = \Omega^c$ is in $\mathcal{F}$. Countable intersections are in $\mathcal{F}$ by De Morgan: $\bigcap A_n = \bigl(\bigcup A_n^c\bigr)^c$. Differences $A \setminus B = A \cap B^c$ are in $\mathcal{F}$. Starting from measurable sets, any construction that uses countably many set operations ends in a measurable set.</p>
    <p>Three examples:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>trivial</strong> σ-algebra $\{\varnothing, \Omega\}$, the smallest possible.</li>
      <li>The <strong>power set</strong>, all subsets of $\Omega$, the largest. When $\Omega$ is finite or countable, this is always the choice, and nothing is lost.</li>
      <li>A σ-algebra <strong>generated by a partition</strong>. Split $\Omega$ into disjoint cells $G_1, \ldots, G_k$ that together cover it. The unions of cells, including the empty union $\varnothing$, form a σ-algebra with $2^k$ sets.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="information">1.5 A σ-algebra is information</h2>
    <p>Toss a coin twice. The set of outcomes is $\Omega = \{HH, HT, TH, TT\}$. Consider three observers.</p>
    <p>The first observer has seen nothing. The only events whose occurrence they can confirm or rule out are $\Omega$ (something happened) and $\varnothing$. Their σ-algebra is $\mathcal{F}_0 = \{\varnothing, \Omega\}$.</p>
    <p>The second has seen the first toss. They can answer “did the event $\{HH, HT\}$ happen?” (was the first toss a head?), and the same for its complement $\{TH, TT\}$. They cannot answer “was there exactly one head?”, because $\{HT, TH\}$ splits across the information they have. Their σ-algebra is $\mathcal{F}_1 = \{\varnothing, \{HH, HT\}, \{TH, TT\}, \Omega\}$, generated by the partition into “first toss H” and “first toss T.”</p>
    <p>The third has seen both tosses and can answer every question. Their σ-algebra $\mathcal{F}_2$ is the power set, with $2^4 = 16$ events.</p>
    <figure>
<!--fig:coins-->
      <figcaption>Figure 1.1: three states of knowledge about two coin tosses. Each σ-algebra consists of all unions of the outlined cells. More information means finer cells and more events.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>The reading to keep: <strong>$A \in \mathcal{G}$ means that someone holding the information $\mathcal{G}$ can tell whether $A$ happened.</strong> The three axioms then say something sensible. If you can tell whether $A$ happened, you can tell whether it did not (complements). If you can tell, for each $n$, whether $A_n$ happened, you can tell whether at least one did (countable unions). The inclusions $\mathcal{F}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{F}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{F}_2$ say that information accumulates and is never forgotten. A sequence of σ-algebras increasing in this way is a <strong>filtration</strong>, and it is how macroeconomics represents the flow of information over time. Chapter 5 is built on it.</p>
    <p>For most σ-algebras one cannot list the sets. They are described by what they are generated from. Given any collection $\mathcal{C}$ of subsets, the <strong>σ-algebra generated by</strong> $\mathcal{C}$, written $\sigma(\mathcal{C})$, is the smallest σ-algebra containing every set in $\mathcal{C}$. It exists because the intersection of any family of σ-algebras is again a σ-algebra, so one can intersect all σ-algebras that contain $\mathcal{C}$. Informally, $\sigma(\mathcal{C})$ is the information of someone who knows, for every $C \in \mathcal{C}$, whether $C$ happened, and has drawn every logical consequence.</p>
    <p>The most important example is the <strong>Borel σ-algebra</strong> $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})$, generated by the intervals. It contains every open and closed set, every single point $\{x\} = \bigcap_n (x - \frac1n, x]$, every countable set, the rationals, the irrationals, and every set you will ever write down with inequalities and limits. Its sets are the <strong>Borel sets</strong>. $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is generated the same way by rectangles $(a_1, b_1] \times \cdots \times (a_n, b_n]$. When the state space of a model is a subset of $\mathbb{R}^n$, its σ-algebra is the Borel σ-algebra unless something else is said.</p>

    <h2 id="measures">1.6 Measures</h2>
    <p><strong>Definition.</strong> A <strong>measure</strong> on a measurable space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F})$ is a function $\mu : \mathcal{F} \to [0, \infty]$ with $\mu(\varnothing) = 0$ that is countably additive. The triple $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)$ is a <strong>measure space</strong>. If $\mu(\Omega) = 1$, it is a <strong>probability measure</strong>, usually written $P$, and $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P)$ is a <strong>probability space</strong>.</p>
    <p>Examples, each of which appears in macro models:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Counting measure</strong>: $\mu(A)$ is the number of elements of $A$. On $\mathbb{N}$, integrating against it will give ordinary sums.</li>
      <li><strong>Point mass</strong> (Dirac measure) at $x$: $\delta_x(A) = 1$ if $x \in A$ and $0$ otherwise. All the mass sits at one point. A cohort of newborn households that all start with zero assets has initial distribution $\delta_0$.</li>
      <li><strong>Discrete distributions</strong>: $\mu = \sum_k p_k \delta_{x_k}$ with $p_k \geq 0$ and $\sum_k p_k = 1$, meaning $\mu(A) = \sum_{k : x_k \in A} p_k$. A Markov chain for employment status lives here.</li>
      <li><strong>Lebesgue measure</strong> $\lambda$ on $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}))$: length. Its defining property is $\lambda((a, b]) = b - a$.</li>
      <li><strong>Distributions with a density</strong>: $\mu(A) = \int_A f(x)\,dx$ for a nonnegative $f$ integrating to $1$. The normal distribution of a productivity shock is one.</li>
      <li><strong>A population</strong>: $\Omega = [0, 1]$ with Lebesgue measure, interpreted as a set of households. $\lambda(A)$ is the fraction of households in $A$.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The last example has total mass $1$ and so is formally a probability measure, though nothing in it is random. The same mathematics serves two readings. “The fraction of households with property $X$” and “the probability that a randomly drawn household has property $X$” are the same number. Heterogeneous-agent macroeconomics moves between the two readings constantly, and Chapter 7 examines when that is legitimate.</p>
    <p>Lebesgue measure deserves a remark. The definition only fixes $\lambda$ on intervals. Does a measure with $\lambda((a,b]) = b-a$ exist on all Borel sets, and is it unique? Both answers are yes, by the following theorem, stated without proof.</p>
    <p><strong>Theorem</strong> (Carathéodory extension). Suppose $\mu_0$ is defined on a collection of sets closed under finite intersections that generates $\mathcal{F}$, and is countably additive there. Then $\mu_0$ extends to a measure on $\mathcal{F}$. If $\Omega$ is a countable union of sets of finite $\mu_0$-measure, the extension is unique.</p>
    <p>The practical consequence for an economist is this. A probability measure on $\mathbb{R}$ is completely determined by its values on half-lines $(-\infty, x]$, that is, by its <strong>distribution function</strong> $F(x) = \mu((-\infty, x])$. Two distributions with the same distribution function are the same distribution. When a paper reports a wealth distribution by its distribution function, nothing has been lost.</p>
    <p>Three properties of measures follow quickly from the definition and are used constantly.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Monotonicity</strong>: if $A \subseteq B$, then $\mu(A) \leq \mu(B)$. Write $B$ as the disjoint union of $A$ and $B \setminus A$.</li>
      <li><strong>Countable subadditivity</strong>: $\mu\bigl(\bigcup_n A_n\bigr) \leq \sum_n \mu(A_n)$ for any sets, disjoint or not. Overlaps are counted more than once on the right.</li>
      <li><strong>Continuity</strong>: if $A_1 \subseteq A_2 \subseteq \cdots$ and $A = \bigcup_n A_n$, then $\mu(A_n) \to \mu(A)$. If $A_1 \supseteq A_2 \supseteq \cdots$, $A = \bigcap_n A_n$, and $\mu(A_1) \lt \infty$, then also $\mu(A_n) \to \mu(A)$.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Continuity from below is countable additivity in disguise. Peel the increasing sets into disjoint rings $B_1 = A_1$, $B_n = A_n \setminus A_{n-1}$. Then $\mu(A_n) = \sum_{k \leq n} \mu(B_k)$, a partial sum of a series whose total is $\mu(A)$. As an example, the probability that unemployment exceeds $10$ percent at some date up to $T$ increases with $T$, and its limit as $T \to \infty$ is the probability that unemployment ever exceeds $10$ percent. The finiteness condition for decreasing sets is needed: $A_n = [n, \infty)$ has $\lambda(A_n) = \infty$ for every $n$, yet $\bigcap_n A_n = \varnothing$.</p>

    <h2 id="null">1.7 Sets of measure zero</h2>
    <p>A set $N$ with $\mu(N) = 0$ is a <strong>null set</strong>. Under Lebesgue measure every single point is null, since $\{x\} \subseteq (x - \varepsilon, x]$ for every $\varepsilon \gt 0$, and so $\lambda(\{x\}) \leq \varepsilon$. By countable additivity, every countable set is null. In particular the rationals have length zero, even though between any two real numbers there is a rational.</p>
    <p>Figure 1.2 shows the same fact directly. List the rationals in $[0,1]$ as $q_1, q_2, \ldots$ and cover $q_n$ by an interval of width $\varepsilon/2^n$. Every rational is covered, and the total width of the cover is at most $\varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/4 + \cdots = \varepsilon$. Since $\varepsilon$ can be as small as we like, the rationals fit inside sets of arbitrarily small total length.</p>
    <figure>
<!--fig:cover-->
      <figcaption>Figure 1.2: covering the rationals. The $n$-th rational gets an interval of width $\varepsilon/2^n$, so all of them together fit into total width $\varepsilon$. (Widths are drawn to scale with $\varepsilon = 1/2$.)</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Not every null set is countable. The Cantor set, obtained by repeatedly deleting middle thirds of $[0,1]$, is uncountable and has length zero. Such sets do not appear in economics, but they are a reminder that “small” in the sense of measure and “small” in the sense of counting are different things.</p>
    <p>A property holds <strong>almost everywhere</strong> (a.e.), or <strong>almost surely</strong> (a.s.) when $\mu$ is a probability, if the set where it fails is contained in a null set. Two functions that are equal almost everywhere are, for every purpose in these notes, the same function. This language matters in economics for several reasons.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>In a continuum economy, changing the behaviour of a null set of households changes no aggregate. An equilibrium in which every household optimises and one in which almost every household optimises have the same prices and quantities. Statements about individual behaviour in such models are statements “for almost every $i$.”</li>
      <li>An Euler equation like the one in Section 1.1 is an equality between two random variables. It holds almost surely: in every history, except possibly a set of histories of probability zero.</li>
      <li>A distribution with a density gives every single point probability zero. Under a normal shock, the event “$\varepsilon = 0$ exactly” never matters. A distribution in which a single point carries positive mass is different, and Chapter 4 shows why the wealth distribution in an incomplete-markets model is of that kind.</li>
    </ul>

